      "id": 1,
      "claim": "For sound in a stationary medium the observed frequency is f' = f * (v + v_o) / (v - v_s), where v is the speed of sound, v_o is the observer velocity component toward the source, and v_s is the source velocity component toward the observer.",
      "evidence": "Standard Doppler‑effect formula for waves that propagate in a material medium (e.g., Halliday & Resnick, Physics).",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "No perceived Doppler shift occurs when f' = f, which requires the numerator and denominator of the fraction to be equal, i.e., v + v_o = v - v_s → v_o = -v_s when the velocities are defined with opposite signs, or equivalently the source and observer have identical velocity vectors relative to the medium.",
      "evidence": "Algebraic manipulation of the formula in step 1; physically, a zero relative radial velocity between source and observer yields no frequency change.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "In option (C) both source and detector travel in the same direction, so they can be assigned equal speeds; this makes their velocity vectors identical, satisfying the condition from step 2 and giving f' = f.",
      "evidence": "If v_source = v_observer (same magnitude and direction), then the relative radial velocity is zero, so the Doppler factor equals 1.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "In options (A), (B) and (D) the source and observer cannot have identical velocity vectors: (A) observer is stationary while source moves, (B) source is stationary while observer moves, (D) they move in opposite directions, so the relative radial velocity is non‑zero and a Doppler shift must occur.",
      "evidence": "Each case violates the equality required in step 2; the relative speed is |v_s|, |v_o|, or |v_s|+|v_o| respectively, all > 0.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    }
  ],
